  "notes": "The arch-producer of pop maximalism, windowed to 1990+ exactly as the research does — the 1980s work is out of scope, which also clears Gate 2. 88–120 straight, backbeat on 2&4. Harmony is \"minor tonic with a static pedal — i–VI–III–VII cycles that never cadence; the harmony is a bed for texture rather than a narrative\", so no family contains a V and the harmonic rhythm sits on two bars a chord. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"hard gated kick on 1 and 3&\", \"distorted snare/clap on 2&4\", and \"16th noise-hat\" with \"reversed-cymbal risers as **section markers**\" — that last phrase is why the `reverse` and `riser` lanes lead the percussion while the fill schedule is wide: the marker is not a fill. The method is the entry's real content: \"**re-recording everything live and then editing**\", and on the 2002 crossover he re-cut every instrumental track and, with the singers gone, \"duplicated and pitch-shifted vocal lines in software to build the harmony\". That is why the counter runs octave-echo at a high ratio, and why \"two live instruments (piano and electric guitar) carry a track that sounds fully electronic\" puts the bass on `independent_riff` doubling a piano left hand. \"The drama comes from layer-count, not from key changes\", which is the flat harmony against a busy arrangement. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it medium-high and marks anything outside the documented 2002 record **[LOW-CONF]**. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and `bpm.mode` 100.",
